# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious package, for scanner tests.
# Never extract and run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: ultralytics-8.3.41
title: "ultralytics 8.3.41 (PyPI): XMRig cryptominer launched when a YOLO model is created"
campaign: ultralytics-ci-poisoning-2024
date: "2024-12-04"
kind: wheel
ecosystem: pip
name: ultralytics
version: "8.3.41"
benignVersion: "8.3.40"
summary: >-
  The popular computer-vision library ultralytics (YOLO) published 8.3.41 on
  2024-12-04 and 8.3.42 the next day from its own, Trusted-Publishing release
  workflow, with valid Sigstore attestations: an attacker had injected a shell
  command through a pull-request branch name into a pull_request_target
  workflow, stolen the GitHub Actions cache token and most likely poisoned the
  pip cache the release job restored. The injected code was never in the
  repository. Creating a YOLO model downloaded an XMRig miner to
  /tmp/ultralytics_runner (fetched as a Git blob through the GitHub API, a new
  gitApi mode of safe_download), started it detached with setsid and its output
  discarded, pointed at a mining pool, and deleted the file. 8.3.45 and 8.3.46,
  pushed later with a leaked API token, exfiltrated the environment and ran
  XMRig straight from the package's __init__.py.
techniques: [ci-cache-poisoning, runtime-trigger, cryptominer, download-and-execute, process-hiding]
reconstruction: >-
  models/yolo/model.py and utils/downloads.py follow HiddenLayer's excerpts of
  8.3.41: the platform check in YOLO.__init__ choosing a Git blob ID per
  operating system, safe_download(..., file="/tmp/ultralytics_runner",
  gitApi=True), and safe_run(), which chmods the file, starts it with the
  wallet, pool and -k arguments under setsid with all output discarded, and
  removes it. The pool host and the GitHub API host are defanged (".invalid"),
  the wallet and blob IDs are random stand-ins of the same form, and the rest
  of the library is a small stand-in for the real one.
references:
  - https://www.hiddenlayer.com/research/ultralytics-python-package-compromise-deploys-cryptominer
  - https://blog.pypi.org/posts/2024-12-11-ultralytics-attack-analysis/
  - https://blog.yossarian.net/2024/12/06/zizmor-ultralytics-injection
  - https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics/issues/18027
  - https://www.reversinglabs.com/blog/compromised-ultralytics-pypi-package-delivers-crypto-coinminer
legitimateHosts:
  - github.com
indicators:
  - ultralytics_runner
  - safe_run
  - consrensys
